    "updated_at": "2026-11-08T15:19:07Z",
    "body": "Triage, checked on `main` 8b0d784.\n\n**What exists:** CAMCraft ships a built-in catalog of 28 metric tools (`tools::catalog`: end mills 2-12 mm, ball and bull noses, drills, chamfer mills, M4/M6 taps, a boring bar, the lathe inserts, a probe). Each document keeps its own tool list. There's no import or export of a library file. The closest thing to a CAMCraft tool format is the `definition` object the MCP server already speaks: `list_tool_catalog` returns it and `add_tool` takes it (kind, diameter, corner_r, flutes, flute_len, shank and holder sizes, rpm, feed, material).\n\n**A native format first**, as the report suggests: export the document's tools as a JSON array of `{name, definition}`, the same shape MCP uses, and import that file back. That settles field mapping and required fields in one place: import can refuse a tool that `Tool::is_sane` refuses, by name. A duplicate name could be skipped or renamed, but never silently overwrite a tool an operation uses.\n\n**Vendor formats:** the Bantam download is a Fusion 360 `.json` and an Inventor HSM / HSMWorks `.hsmlib`, both Autodesk formats. AGENTS.md's Clean-Room Protocol allows only Autodesk's published user documentation (behaviour only) as a reference, and says tool libraries are \"made from scratch\". Whether an importer for those formats is allowed, and from what source, is the owner's decision before anyone writes one. Manufacturers' published cutting data (Harvey, Sandvik, Lakeshore) is plain data and could be entered in the native format.\n\n**Generic starter tools:** the shipped catalog is metric only, so an inch (SAE) set would be a data addition to the same catalog.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
